<!--Post: 7780--><!--Time: 1108681011-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7780"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Itty<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Itty<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, February 17, 2005 - 2:56 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Max, Im sorry, I have confused things even further. I have 2 scenarios, one that has the &#34;b&#34; the &#34;T&#34; and the &#34;k&#34; superimposed over each other, and includes known victims. <BR>that is the one I was explaining above. I think to finish the capital &#34;T&#34; in this scenario, we need to find a crime around the intersection of S West street and 400 &#40;which is also kellogg&#41; <BR> <BR>second scenario, the letters would be separate,and this includes victims we are not sure about, like Mould Hedge and Davis, and you are correct, we have nothing yet to make the last leg of the &#34;k&#34;. There is a road right out there in the correct spot called &#34;diamond&#34; street or road, That would be my guess as to where we should look for a crime scene to finish the &#34;k&#34; <BR> <BR>I cant believe how much difficulty I am having explaining this, I feel like a &#39;tard.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
